.TH CHTTPSVR_START 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_start \- register a listener with the running engine
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <chttpserver.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpsvr_start(chttpsvr srv,
                             const chttpsvr_config_t *cfg);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Starts the server on the port and host specified in cfg.  cfg may be
NULL, in which case the defaults from CHTTPSVR_CONFIG_DEFAULT are used.

cfg->host may be a plain address ("0.0.0.0", "127.0.0.1", ...) for a
TCP listener, or a "unix://path/to/socket" string to bind a Unix
domain socket at that path instead (cfg->port is then ignored; a stale
socket file already at that path is removed automatically before
binding).  A single chttpsvr instance listens on either TCP or a Unix
socket, never both; create two instances for both.

If the shared, process-wide event_loop reactor (and its companion
idle-timeout sweep thread) is not yet running in this process, it is
started implicitly on this call and this call blocks until it is
confirmed running before proceeding.  This reactor is independent of
chttpclient's own engine; starting or stopping one has no effect on
the other.  The listener socket is then bound synchronously and the
function returns once the socket is in LISTEN state and registered
with the reactor.

Multiple chttpsvr handles may be started on different ports (or Unix
socket paths) simultaneously, all sharing the same reactor.

Port 0 is rejected with ccol_invalid_args, unless cfg->host is a
"unix://" path (where port is meaningless and ignored).

A ctpool for dispatching all handlers (buffered and streaming) is
created at start time using cfg->worker_thread_count threads and a
queue whose capacity is cfg->worker_queue_capacity (0 = 1024 * threads;
CHTTPSVR_QUEUE_UNBOUNDED = no capacity limit).  The pool is owned by
the server and destroyed in __chttpsvr_destroy.

Route rejections: an unmatched/malformed route (404/405/500, decided
before any body byte is read) and a matched route whose ctpool is at
capacity (503) are both rejected with a courtesy error response whose
write is carried out on a small dedicated pool of its own, never the
sole reactor thread; a slow-reading client being told "no" therefore
cannot delay dispatch for any other, unrelated connection.

Body size limit: cfg->max_body_size caps the request body; 0 means
unlimited.  A buffered route whose body exceeds this is rejected with
413 before the handler ever runs; a streaming route's handler is
always invoked, and chttpsvr_req_read() returns -1 with
chttpsvr_req_stream_error() == ccol_msg_too_large once the limit is
crossed.  Either way the connection is closed after the resulting
response (Connection: close) rather than kept alive, since the excess
body bytes beyond the limit are discarded, not drained.

A buffered route whose declared Content-Length already exceeds
max_body_size is rejected with 413 immediately, before ever waiting for
a body byte (which, for a stalled or malicious peer, may never arrive);
this does not apply to a streaming route, whose handler is still always
invoked regardless of the declared Content-Length, matching its own
documented contract above.  Independently of route kind, any single
chunk (chunked Transfer-Encoding) whose declared size exceeds
max_body_size is also rejected as soon as its chunk-size line is parsed,
before waiting for that chunk's data; this surfaces the same way any
other malformed body already does for the route's kind (413/
ccol_msg_too_large).

Body read timeouts: cfg->stream_read_timeout_ms bounds how long a
worker will wait for the next batch of body bytes (buffered or
streaming); 0 waits indefinitely.  Because it resets on any new byte,
a client that trickles data just fast enough never trips it, so
cfg->max_body_read_duration_ms (0 = disabled, the default) separately
bounds the *total* time spent reading one request's body regardless of
per-gap progress; see chttpsvr_req_read.  cfg->response_write_timeout_ms
(0 = use stream_read_timeout_ms's value) bounds the analogous per-call
wait on the response-write side, protecting a worker thread from a
slow-reading client during response send; cfg->max_response_write_
duration_ms (0 = disabled, the default) is the write-side analogue of
max_body_read_duration_ms, separately bounding the *total* time spent
sending one response regardless of per-call progress.  On expiry the
response send fails and the connection is closed.

Connection idle timeout: cfg->read_timeout_ms/idle_timeout_ms are
enforced by the module-local idle-timeout sweep thread (shared by
every chttpsvr instance in the process), not by any per-connection OS
timer.  When idle_timeout_ms is non-zero it takes precedence over
read_timeout_ms; otherwise read_timeout_ms is used; 0 for both
disables idle-timeout enforcement entirely.  This only bounds time
spent idle between requests (awaiting the next pipelined/keep-alive
request), never time spent actively being handled by a worker thread.

Header size limit: cfg->max_header_bytes (0 = library default, 64 KiB)
bounds the combined size of one request's header block; a request
whose headers exceed this has its connection closed outright (no HTTP
response is sent, matching every other pre-routing parse error this
module's request parser reports).

Connection count limit: cfg->max_connections (0 = unlimited) bounds
how many connections this listener may have open at once; once at
capacity, new connections are simply left pending in the kernel's own
listen backlog rather than accepted and immediately rejected, and the
listener itself stops accepting until a slot frees, resuming
automatically with a worst case of about one second between a slot
freeing and the listener noticing.

Transient resource exhaustion (the process or system temporarily out
of file descriptors, or a custom allocator supplied via
create_chttpsvr_mp temporarily unable to satisfy a per-connection
allocation) pauses this listener the same way: new connections are
left pending until a periodic sweep resumes the listener, with the
same worst-case one-second latency. This keeps a transient shortage on
one listener from delaying any other, unrelated connection on any
other chttpsvr instance sharing the same reactor thread.

Socket options (all default false, all best-effort, all no-ops for a
Unix domain socket listener): cfg->enable_keepalive sets SO_KEEPALIVE
on every accepted connection. cfg->enable_reuseport sets SO_REUSEPORT
on the listening socket, letting multiple chttpsvr instances bind the
identical host:port for kernel-load-balanced accept. cfg->ipv6_only
sets IPV6_V6ONLY on an AF_INET6 listener, so it does not also
implicitly accept IPv4-mapped connections.

Restart: after chttpsvr_stop is called, chttpsvr_start may be called
again on the same handle to register a new listener.  The old ctpool is
drained and replaced with a fresh one.  Any TLS context from the
previous cycle is freed automatically before the new one is installed.

Calling chttpsvr_start from within one of srv's own request handlers or
middleware to restart the very server that handler is running on
(typically preceded by that same handler calling chttpsvr_stop) is
refused with ccol_not_permitted rather than attempted: that thread's
own in-flight request can never finish while it is itself blocked
draining that exact request, so the restart could never safely
proceed.  Refusing it leaves srv in a perfectly safe, recoverable state
(its previous configuration, or, if chttpsvr_stop already ran, simply
stopped); a later, legitimate call to chttpsvr_start from a different
thread still restarts it normally.

TLS: cfg->tls, when non-NULL, must have both cert_path and key_path
set; any other shape (exactly one of the two set, cfg->tls->ca_bundle_path
set with neither, or cfg->tls left otherwise empty, e.g. CHTTP_TLS_DEFAULT)
is rejected with ccol_invalid_args before any other work is attempted,
rather than silently starting the server as plain, unencrypted HTTP.  A
trust store, or an otherwise-empty chttp_tls_config_t, is never valid
server-side configuration without a server identity certificate (unlike
the client side, where ca_bundle_path alone is the ordinary way to
configure custom-CA verification with no client certificate involved).
If cert_path/key_path are both set but the certificate/key pair cannot
be loaded (a missing/unreadable file or malformed contents),
chttpsvr_start fails with ccol_unexpected_failure and no listener is
registered.  If cfg->tls->ca_bundle_path is also set, it must load
successfully too (this is what enables mutual-TLS client-certificate
verification on the resulting listener); a failure to load it is
likewise reported as ccol_unexpected_failure rather than silently
starting the server without the client-certificate enforcement that
was requested.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success on success.
ccol_invalid_args if srv is CHTTPSVR_INVALID, a stale/already-destroyed
handle, if cfg->port is 0 and cfg->host is not a "unix://" path, or if
cfg->tls is set without both cert_path and key_path also set.
ccol_not_permitted if this server is already started, or if this call
was made from within one of srv's own request handlers/middleware.
ccol_not_enough_memory if memory allocation fails, or if the shared
engine (its reactor threads or its own diagnostics logger) could not
be created.
ccol_unexpected_failure if the listen socket could not be created/bound
or registered with the shared reactor, if the shared idle-timeout
sweep thread could not be started, or if cfg->tls was set but its
certificate/key pair or ca_bundle_path could not be loaded.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
chttpsvr_stop only closes the listener socket; connections already
accepted before the stop keep running and may still dispatch further
requests on this same handle at any point during a subsequent restart.
The old ctpool is only drained and destroyed once every request already
in flight has completed, so a restart is safe to call even while such a
connection is still active; no request can ever be handed to a ctpool
that is concurrently being torn down. This wait is bounded (up to 30
seconds, plus a further short grace period), even for a connection
whose worker thread is genuinely blocked reading or writing it under a
0 ("wait indefinitely") stream_read_timeout_ms/max_body_read_duration_ms/
response_write_timeout_ms/max_response_write_duration_ms configuration;
see chttpsvr_config_t's own
documentation (CHTTPSVR_CONFIG_DEFAULT(3)) for the mechanism.

A chttpsvr_start call restarting srv is also safe to race against a
concurrent chttpsvr_stop call on the same handle from a different
thread: this function waits for that in-flight stop's own teardown of
the old listener to fully finish before binding a new one on the same
host/port, rather than racing a bind() against a socket the stop has
not yet closed.

chttpsvr_start is also safe to race against a concurrent, engine-wide
teardown (chttpsvr_engine_stop(), or the shared reactor's own graceful
shutdown when the last other server referencing it is destroyed): it
transparently retries until that teardown has finished, rather than
racing it.
.fi
